<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The goal of this paper is to prove the log-concavity of the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of paving matroids.
Elias, Proudfoot, and Wakefield <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> introduced the notion of the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial of a matroid.
Similarly, Gao and Xie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> introduced the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial of a matroid.
Recently, Braden, Huh, Matherne, Proudfoot, and Wang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> proved that the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials and the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of matroids have only non-negative coefficients.
However, the log-concavity of Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials and inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of matroids is still open and only a few results have been obtained, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.
A sequence <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">log-concave and has no internal zeros</span> if it satisfies <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}^{2}\geq a_{i-1}a_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}^{2}\geq a_{i-1}a_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the indices of its nonzero elements form consecutive integers. A polynomial is said to be log-concave if its coefficients form a log-concave sequence. Gao and Xie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> conjectured that the coefficients of the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of matroids are log-concave and have no internal zeros. We use <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial of the matroid <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficients of <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> form a log-concave sequence and have no internal zeros.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Elias, Proudfoot, and Wakefield <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> conjectured that the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials have only real roots. However, Gao and Xie pointed out that inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials do not necessarily have only real roots. Nevertheless, we conjecture that a variation of inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials has only real roots.
Recall that a matroid is called <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">paving</em> if the size of every circuit is at least the rank of <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.
The main object of this paper is as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any paving matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficients of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> form a log-concave sequence and have no internal zeros.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> via the approach of real-rooted polynomials.
Given a polynomial of degree <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)=a_{0}+a_{1}t+\cdots+a_{n}t^{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)=a_{0}+a_{1}t+\cdots+a_{n}t^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">we denote</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(p(t))=\binom{n}{0}a_{0}+\binom{n}{1}a_{1}t+\cdots+\binom{n}{n}a_{n}t^{n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(p(t))=\binom{n}{0}a_{0}+\binom{n}{1}a_{1}t+\cdots+\binom{n}{n}a_{n}t^{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which is the Hadamard product of <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+t)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+t)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that the operator <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B</annotation></semantics></math> is not linear for two polynomials with distinct degrees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any paving matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomial <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{M}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Q</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{M}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By Newton’s inequalities, Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> implies Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.
In general, we make the following conjecture, which would imply Conjecture <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Conjecture 1.1 ([]). ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any simple matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomial <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{M}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Q</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{M}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that a sequence satisfying the Turán inequality is also called a log-concave sequence.
The higher order Turán inequalities are defined by
<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4(a_{i}^{2}-a_{i-1}a_{i+1})(a_{i+1}^{2}-a_{i}a_{i+2})-(a_{i}a_{i+1}-a_{i-1}a_{i+2})^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4(a_{i}^{2}-a_{i-1}a_{i+1})(a_{i+1}^{2}-a_{i}a_{i+2})-(a_{i}a_{i+1}-a_{i-1}a_{i+2})^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
In recent years, the higher-order Turán inequalities for combinatorial sequences have attracted significant attention, as seen in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.
A theorem of Mařík <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> states that if a real polynomial
<math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{0}a_{0}+\binom{n}{1}a_{1}t+\cdots+\binom{n}{n}a_{n}t^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{0}a_{0}+\binom{n}{1}a_{1}t+\cdots+\binom{n}{n}a_{n}t^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots, then <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},a_{1},\ldots,a_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},a_{1},\ldots,a_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the higher-order Turán inequalities.
By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, we get the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any paving matroid <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the coefficients of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy the higher order Turán inequalities.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The structure of this paper is as follows.
In Section 2, we provide the necessary background on real-rooted polynomials.
Section 3 focuses on the log-concavity of the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of paving matroids, presenting the main proofs and results.
Finally, in Section 4, we propose several conjectures regarding the degree of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Interlacing and multiplier sequence</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we recall some useful results of interlacing polynomials and multiplier sequences.
Given two real-rooted polynomials <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> with positive leading coefficients, let <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{u_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of roots of <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of roots of <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> interlaces <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)\ll f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)\ll f(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, if either of the following two conditions is satisfied:</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg f(t)=\deg g(t)=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg f(t)=\deg g(t)=n</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{n}\leq u_{n}\leq v_{n-1}\leq\cdots\leq v_{2}\leq u_{2}\leq v_{1}\leq u_{1};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{n}\leq u_{n}\leq v_{n-1}\leq\cdots\leq v_{2}\leq u_{2}\leq v_{1}\leq u_{1};</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg f(t)=\deg g(t)+1=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg f(t)=\deg g(t)+1=n</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle u_{n}\leq v_{n-1}\leq\cdots\leq v_{2}\leq u_{2}\leq v_{1}\leq u_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle u_{n}\leq v_{n-1}\leq\cdots\leq v_{2}\leq u_{2}\leq v_{1}\leq u_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For convenience, we let <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\ll f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≪</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\ll f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\ll 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≪</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\ll 0</annotation></semantics></math> for any real-rooted polynomial <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following Brändén <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>,
a sequence of real polynomials <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{1},\dots,f_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{1},\dots,f_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>
with positive leading coefficients is said to be <em id="S2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interlacing</em> if <math id="S2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i}\ll f_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i}\ll f_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i&lt;j\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i&lt;j\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>.
The following lemma plays an important role in our paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, Proposition 3.3)<span id="S2.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0},f_{1},f_{2},\ldots,f_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0},f_{1},f_{2},\ldots,f_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> are real-rooted polynomials with <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}\ll f_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}\ll f_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}\ll f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}\ll f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>, then the polynomial sequence <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> is interlacing.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also need the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, Proposition 3.5)<span id="S2.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> be real-rooted polynomials with nonnegative coefficients.</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\ll g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\ll g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\ll h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\ll h</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\ll g+h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\ll g+h</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\ll g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\ll g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\ll g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\ll g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then </span><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f+h\ll g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f+h\ll g</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also say that the roots of <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are alternating if <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)\ll g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)\ll g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)\ll f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)\ll f(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The main tool we use to prove the property of alternating roots is the following Hermite-Kakeya-Obreschkoff theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Hermite-Kakeya-Obreschkoff, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 2.4]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(t)</annotation></semantics></math> be two polynomials with real coefficients. The roots of <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are
alternating if and only if for any real numbers <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math>, the polynomial <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda p(t)+\mu q(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda p(t)+\mu q(t)</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, in this form of the Hermite-Kakeya-Obreschkoff theorem, we cannot determine whether <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> interlaces <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> interlaces <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. To address this, we next recall the Wrońskian notation of two polynomials.
The Wrońskian of two polynomials <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[f,g]=f^{\prime}g-fg^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[f,g]=f^{\prime}g-fg^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is easy to see that for any pair of polynomials <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math> with interlacing roots, the Wrońskian <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[f,g]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[f,g]</annotation></semantics></math> is either nonnegative on all of <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> or nonpositive on all of <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, if <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[f,g]\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[f,g]\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\ll g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\ll g</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, P. 4]</cite>). Therefore, we only need to check the signs of <math id="S2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W[f,g]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W[f,g]</annotation></semantics></math> for certain values of <math id="S2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
‘</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefn1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefn1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefn1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefn1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefn1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefn1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A sequence <math id="S2.Thmdefn1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> of real numbers is called a <em id="S2.Thmdefn1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">multiplier sequence</em> if, whenever any real polynomial
<math id="S2.Thmdefn1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_{k}t^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}a_{k}t^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots, so does the polynomial <math id="S2.Thmdefn1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma[f(t)]=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\gamma_{k}a_{k}t^{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma[f(t)]=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\gamma_{k}a_{k}t^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefn2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defn">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefn2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefn2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A sequence <math id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\{\gamma_{i}\}_{k=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\{\gamma_{i}\}_{k=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is called an <em id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright"><math id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-sequence</em> if for every polynomial <math id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> of degree less than or equal to <math id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and with only real roots, the polynomial <math id="S2.Thmdefn2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma[f(t)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma[f(t)]</annotation></semantics></math> also has only real roots.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Pólya-Schur, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 3.3]</cite>)<span id="S2.Thmthm4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of real numbers. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiplier sequence if and only if the roots of the polynomial <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma[(1+t)^{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma[(1+t)^{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> are all real and of the same sign for all integers <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, Algebraic Characterization of <math id="S2.Thmthm5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-sequences)<span id="S2.Thmthm5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma=\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\{\gamma_{k}\}_{k=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sequence of real numbers. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-sequence if and only if the roots of the polynomial
<math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma[(1+t)^{n}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma[(1+t)^{n}]</annotation></semantics></math> are all real and of the same sign.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following two lemmas will be used frequently later. Although they are well-known and the proofs are straightforward, we provide them here for the reader’s convenience</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For positive integers <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq l\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq l\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}\right\}_{i=0}^{\infty}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}\right\}_{i=0}^{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a multiplier sequence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">It is clear that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}=\frac{\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}{(i+k)!}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}=\frac{\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}{(i+k)!}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">Applying a classical theorem due to Laguerre to the function <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{\Gamma(t+1+k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{\Gamma(t+1+k)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(t+j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(t+j)}</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to see that both <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{(i+k)!}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{(i+k)!}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}</annotation></semantics></math> are multiplier sequences, see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, p. 270]</cite> or a more recent literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Theorem 4.1]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For integers <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq l\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq l\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\frac{1}{(d-i)!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(d-i+j)}}\right\}_{i=0}^{d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\frac{1}{(d-i)!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(d-i+j)}}\right\}_{i=0}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-sequence.
In particular, for any nonnegative integer <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\{\frac{1}{(d-i+m)!}\right\}_{i=0}^{d}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\frac{1}{(d-i+m)!}\right\}_{i=0}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is a <math id="S2.Thmthm7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-sequence.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 ([], Algebraic Characterization of 𝑛-sequences). ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we need to show that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{d}\binom{d}{i}\frac{1}{(d-i)!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(d-i+j)}}t^{i}=\sum_{i=0}^{d}\binom{d}{i}\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}t^{d-i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{d}\binom{d}{i}\frac{1}{(d-i)!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(d-i+j)}}t^{i}=\sum_{i=0}^{d}\binom{d}{i}\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}t^{d-i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">has only real roots.
The real-rootedness of the right-hand side of (<a href="#S2.E1" title="In Proof. ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) is equivalent to that of</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i=0}^{d}\binom{d}{i}\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}t^{i}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i=0}^{d}\binom{d}{i}\frac{1}{i!\prod_{j=l}^{k}{(i+j)}}t^{i},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">which follows from Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we often need to prove that certain specific cubic or quadratic polynomials have only real roots. Although the form is consistent, the degree may vary. We require the following fact.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b,c):=18\,abcd-4\,b^{3}d+b^{2}c^{2}-4\,ac^{3}-27\,a^{2}d^{2}&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>27</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b,c):=18\,abcd-4\,b^{3}d+b^{2}c^{2}-4\,ac^{3}-27\,a^{2}d^{2}&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then
the polynomial <math id="S2.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t):=at^{3}+bt^{2}+ct+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t):=at^{3}+bt^{2}+ct+d</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\not=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(a,b,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(a,b,c)</annotation></semantics></math> is just the discriminant of <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the cubic polynomial <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> has three distinct real roots by the definition of the discriminant.
If <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(0,b,c)=b^{2}(c^{2}-4bd)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(0,b,c)=b^{2}(c^{2}-4bd)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\not=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\not=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> have two distinct real roots.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Log-concavity for paving matroid</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section aims to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. Our approach relies on the alternating of roots.
Recall that Gao and Xie <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> obtained the following explicit formula for the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials of any uniform matroid,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{U_{m,d}}(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{\lfloor(d-1)/2\rfloor}\frac{m(d-2i)}{(m+i)(d-i+m)}\binom{m+d}{d}\binom{d}{i}t^{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{U_{m,d}}(t)=\sum_{i=0}^{\lfloor(d-1)/2\rfloor}\frac{m(d-2i)}{(m+i)(d-i+m)}\binom{m+d}{d}\binom{d}{i}t^{i}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Ferroni, Nasr, and Vecchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> obtained the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial of any paving matroid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Theorem 4.4]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a paving matroid of rank <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and cardinality <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m+d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m+d</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> has exactly <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> (stressed) hyperplanes of cardinality <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h+d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h+d-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{M}(t)=Q_{U_{m,d}}(t)-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\ (Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{M}(t)=Q_{U_{m,d}}(t)-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\ (Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of directly proving Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>, we prove</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\ \B\left((Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\ \B\left((Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">has only real roots for any nonnegative numbers <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. To establish this, we will prove the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq h\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq h\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the computational difficulty, we will prove the following lemmas instead of proving Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Log-concavity for paving matroid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any positive integer <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> , we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\B(Q_{U_{1,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{1,d-1}}(t))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\B(Q_{U_{1,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{1,d-1}}(t)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first prove that <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots.
From (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3 Log-concavity for paving matroid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))=\frac{(m+d)!}{(m-1)!}\sum_{i=0}^{\left\lfloor\frac{d-1}{2}\right\rfloor}\frac{(d-2i)}{i!(i+m)(d-i)!(d-i+m)}{\left\lfloor\frac{d-1}{2}\right\rfloor\choose i}t^{i}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><