<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{\left(m^{2}+(d-1)(d+2m-2)+1\right)}{(d+m-1)(d+m)}\binom{d+m-3}{m-1}\binom{d+m}{m+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{\left(m^{2}+(d-1)(d+2m-2)+1\right)}{(d+m-1)(d+m)}\binom{d+m-3}{m-1}\binom{d+m}{m+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p26.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p26.3.1" class="ltx_text">which is negative.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p27.1" class="ltx_p">By combining the above three lemmas and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1 ([], Proposition 3.3). ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we prove Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Log-concavity for paving matroid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Log-concavity for paving matroid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S3.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}=\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}=\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{h}=\B(Q_{U_{m+1-h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{m+1-h,d-1}}(t))\text{\quad for\quad}1\leq h\leq m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{h}=\B(Q_{U_{m+1-h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{m+1-h,d-1}}(t))\text{\quad for\quad}1\leq h\leq m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p28.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p28.2.1" class="ltx_text">It follows from Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Log-concavity for paving matroid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 3.3. ‣ 3 Log-concavity for paving matroid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, and <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3 Log-concavity for paving matroid" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> that
<math id="S3.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}\ll f_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}\ll f_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p28.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{i-1}\ll f_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{i-1}\ll f_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.p28.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the polynomial sequence <math id="S3.p28.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{0},f_{1},\ldots,f_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> is interlacing by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1 ([], Proposition 3.3). ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>.
In particular, we have <math id="S3.p28.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{0}\ll f_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≪</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{0}\ll f_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.p28.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq h\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq h\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))\text{\quad for \quad}1\leq h\leq m." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))\text{\quad for \quad}1\leq h\leq m.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p28.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p28.3.1" class="ltx_text">We complete the proof.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p29.1" class="ltx_p">Although we do not know the specific range of <math id="S3.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, this does not affect us to prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> .</h6>
<div id="S3.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2 ([], Proposition 3.5). ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>,
we have</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\cdot\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≪</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))\ll\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\cdot\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p30.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p30.2.1" class="ltx_text">for any nonnegative real numbers <math id="S3.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the polynomial</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\cdot\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\cdot\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p30.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p30.3.1" class="ltx_text">has only real roots for any nonnegative real numbers <math id="S3.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose that <math id="S3.p30.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a paving matroid.
By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite>, we know that all the coefficients of <math id="S3.p30.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative.
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 ([], Algebraic Characterization of 𝑛-sequences). ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we conclude that the coefficients of <math id="S3.p30.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> forms a <math id="S3.p30.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor\frac{d-1}{2}\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor\frac{d-1}{2}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>-sequence.
By definition, the degree of <math id="S3.p30.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is less than or equal to <math id="S3.p30.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor\frac{d-1}{2}\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor\frac{d-1}{2}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. Again, by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 2.5 ([], Algebraic Characterization of 𝑛-sequences). ‣ 2 Interlacing and multiplier sequence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we know that
<math id="S3.p30.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{M}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{M}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> has only real roots. This completes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.
Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> follows from the well-known Newton’s inequalities.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p31.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that although the degree of <math id="S3.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{U_{m,d}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{U_{m,d}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq d\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq d\leq m</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lfloor(d-1)/2\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lfloor(d-1)/2\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math>, the degree of <math id="S3.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{M}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{M}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> may be less than <math id="S3.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lfloor(d-1)/2\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lfloor(d-1)/2\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this sense, the polynomial <math id="S3.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{M}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{M}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> may not equal</p>
<table id="S3.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\cdot\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{U_{m,d}}(t))-\sum_{h=1}^{m}\lambda_{h}\cdot\B(Q_{U_{h,d}}(t)-Q_{U_{h,d-1}}(t)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p31.2" class="ltx_p">This is why we adopt an indirect argument in the second paragraph of our proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Concluding remarks</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the log-concavity properties established in this paper, we shall study the degrees of the inverse Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomials and propose several intriguing conjectures.
A matroid <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-degenerate</em> if its Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial has degree <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lfloor(\mbox{rk}(M)-1)/2\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>rk</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lfloor(\mbox{rk}(M)-1)/2\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math>, the maximal possible degree, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
Gedeon, Proudfoot, and Young <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> conjectured that there are interlacing properties for the roots of the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial of a non-degenerate matroid <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and those of a contraction <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/{e}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/{e}</annotation></semantics></math> by one element.
However, similar properties do not hold for <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{M}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{M}(t))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Nevertheless, one can still investigate the relationship between the degrees of <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Vecchi <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> obtained the following result for a matroid of odd rank and pointed out that this is not true when <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is of even rank.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a matroid of odd rank <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left[t^{\left\lfloor\frac{r-1}{2}\right\rfloor}\right]P_{M}(t)=\left[t^{\left\lfloor\frac{r-1}{2}\right\rfloor}\right]Q_{M}(t)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left[t^{\left\lfloor\frac{r-1}{2}\right\rfloor}\right]P_{M}(t)=\left[t^{\left\lfloor\frac{r-1}{2}\right\rfloor}\right]Q_{M}(t).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, a matroid <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of odd rank <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is non-degenerate if and only if <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lfloor\frac{r-1}{2}\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lfloor\frac{r-1}{2}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([]). ‣ 4 Concluding remarks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> inspires us to propose the following related conjecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any matroid <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the degrees of <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are the same.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We verified Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Conjecture 4.2. ‣ 4 Concluding remarks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for all matroids on at most 9 elements, as well as for the cycle matroids of graphs with at most 9 vertices.
Braden, Braden, Huh, Matherne, Proudfoot, and Wang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> proved that the coefficients of both <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> are nonnegative.
But are the coefficients strictly positive? We provide
the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any paving matroid <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, each coefficient of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is positive, except possibly the coefficient of <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\lfloor\frac{\text{rk}(M)-1}{2}\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">rk</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\lfloor\frac{\text{rk}(M)-1}{2}\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math>, which may be zero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The linear combination of two real-rooted polynomials <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(t)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, each of degree <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="af(t)+bg(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">af(t)+bg(t)</annotation></semantics></math>. The degree of this combination is either <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, provided that <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{2}+b^{2}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{2}+b^{2}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any paving matroid <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, the degree of <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\B(Q_{M}(t))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\B(Q_{M}(t))</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\lfloor(rk(M)-1)/2-1\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\lfloor(rk(M)-1)/2-1\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math> by the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>. Thus, the coefficient of <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\lfloor(rk(M)-1)/2\rfloor-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\lfloor(rk(M)-1)/2\rfloor-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive. On the other hand, the constant term of <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is just the absolute value of the constant term of the characteristic polynomial of <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, which is positive, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, Proposition 3.1]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, Theorem 7.1.8]</cite>.
It is well known that if the coefficients of a univariate polynomial are non-negative, log-concave, and have no internal zeros, then they are also unimodal.
Thus, for any paving matroid <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, each coefficient of the Kazhdan-Lusztig polynomial <math id="S4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to <math id="S4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;\left\lfloor\frac{\mathrm{rk}(M)-1}{2}\right\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;\left\lfloor\frac{\mathrm{rk}(M)-1}{2}\right\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> is positive.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on Conjecture <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Conjecture 4.2. ‣ 4 Concluding remarks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we believe that an analog of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 4.3. ‣ 4 Concluding remarks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> holds for the Kazhdan–Lusztig polynomials <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> as well. That is, for any paving matroid <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, each coefficient of <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{M}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{M}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> is positive, except possibly the coefficient of <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\lfloor\frac{\mathrm{rk}(M)-1}{2}\rfloor}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>rk</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\lfloor\frac{\mathrm{rk}(M)-1}{2}\rfloor}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
